<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Permits plain HTTP, which Android 9+ blocks by default in release builds.

  WHY THIS IS NEEDED

  The API is a FastAPI server on the developer's laptop, reached over USB
  (adb reverse) or a phone hotspot. It has no TLS certificate and no domain
  name; it is addressed by raw IP on a private network.

  Without this, a RELEASE build fails in a thoroughly confusing way: every
  request dies instantly, the app reports the backend unreachable, and the
  laptop's own logs show no request arriving at all. Debug builds are
  unaffected, because Expo's debug manifest sets usesCleartextTraffic to true,
  so the problem appears only in the build a user actually runs.

  WHY IT IS NOT RESTRICTED TO SPECIFIC ADDRESSES

  A domain-config listing only known hosts would be tighter, and was the first
  instinct. But Android's network security config matches literal hostnames,
  not address ranges, while the backend's address CHANGES: it depends on which
  hotspot or network is in use, which is precisely why the API client probes
  several candidates in turn. Listing some addresses and not others would mean
  a working backend silently unreachable after a network change, with no
  diagnostic. That failure mode is worse than the risk this guards against, on
  an academic prototype talking only to a machine on the same private network.

  THIS SHOULD BE DELETED once the Python backend is embedded in the app via
  Chaquopy: at that point nothing leaves the device and no cleartext is sent
  anywhere at all.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
